# Knowledge Base: Model Evaluation Chapter

## Chapter Context

This chapter asks: you have built a model, can estimate parameters with uncertainty, and can test null hypotheses, but how can you tell if your model is any good? It is a single page with these sections:

1. Goals of model building
2. Model fit vs model adequacy (visualize your data and model; simulation to help the eye; multivariate visualizations)
3. Model diagnostics (interpreting diagnostic plots)
4. Evaluating model fit (R², log-likelihood; model comparison and penalizing additional parameters)
5. Model evaluation: predictive performance (leave-one-out cross-validation; k-fold cross-validation)

Learning goals (from the motivating scenario box at the top of the chapter):

- Distinguish between "rejecting the null" and "having a good alternative"
- Use plots and simulations to judge whether a model captures key patterns in data
- Interpret residual, QQ, and leverage plots to spot nonlinearity, heteroscedasticity, or outliers
- Compare models using adjusted R², AIC, and log-likelihood, and explain how they penalize complexity
- Use cross-validation (LOOCV and k-fold) to assess predictive performance and avoid overfitting
- Reflect on when a more complex model is worth it vs when a simpler one is preferable

The chapter opens with quotes: "All models are wrong, but some are useful" (George Box); "A map is not the territory..." (Alfred Korzybski); "Truth is much too complicated to allow anything but approximations" (John von Neumann). The cross-validation section opens with "With four parameters I can fit an elephant, and with five I can make him wiggle his trunk" (von Neumann to Fermi, according to Freeman Dyson).

